%N A401310 Number of reduced 7 X n Latin rectangles.
%C A401310 A k X n Latin rectangle on the symbols 1, 2, ..., n is called normalized if its first row is 1, 2, ..., n.
%C A401310 A normalized Latin rectangle whose first column is 1, 2, ..., k is called reduced.
%C A401310 a(n) is the number of reduced 7 X n Latin rectangles.
%H A401310 James Staff, <a href="/A401310/b401310.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 7..15</a>
%H A401310 Peter G. Doyle, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0703896">The number of Latin rectangles</a>, arXiv:math/0703896 [math.CO], 2007.
%H A401310 James Staff, <a href="/A401310/a401310.txt">C++ program to compute a(7)-a(15) using the Doyle partition-lattice method, with complete verification transcript</a>
%H A401310 Douglas S. Stones, <a href="https://doi.org/10.37236/487">The many formulae for the number of Latin rectangles</a>, Electronic Journal of Combinatorics 17 (2010), #A1.
%H A401310 Douglas S. Stones and Ian M. Wanless, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcta.2009.03.019">Divisors of the number of Latin rectangles</a>, Journal of Combinatorial Theory, Series A 117 (2010), 204-215.
%H A401310 Rebecca J. Stones, Sheng Lin, Xiaoguang Liu and Gang Wang, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/s00373-015-1643-1">On Computing the Number of Latin Rectangles</a>, Graphs and Combinatorics 32 (2016), 1187-1202.
